    "B": "Research fused permutation generation and formatting. Compare std::next_permutation on the sorted input plus a reusable formatted record against a successor algorithm operating directly on that record's character slots. Fixed brackets, spaces and newline can remain initialized; swaps and suffix reversals need touch only character positions before copying the record into bounded output batches. Ensure the successor scan never forms an iterator before begin, including the final descending permutation. Preserve native char comparison and duplicate suppression; avoid alphabet restrictions or factorial-sized storage. Check record-size arithmetic and retain a streaming fallback for oversized records. Measure whether reduced formatting outweighs strided access and copying, using the unchanged build and evaluator.",
    "C": "Research ordered CPU parallelism, only if threading is supported by the fixed build. Partition the runtime multiset into distinct lexicographically ordered prefix subtrees; each task enumerates its remaining sorted suffix. Adaptively splitting prefixes can expose work despite repeated symbols without computing overflowing factorials. Use at most four execution threads total, with one ordered output writer and bounded producer buffers. Scheduling and backpressure must guarantee that later tasks cannot prevent the earliest unfinished task from progressing. Never retain a complete large subtree's output. Investigate a serial fallback based on runtime workload and repetition, not particular test values; use checked or saturating arithmetic for estimates. Compare serial and parallel versions end-to-end under the unchanged evaluator, including startup and synchronization. If threading is unavailable or slower, retain the serial candidate rather than changing build settings.",
